BanMido wrote:
> Doug Freyburger wrote:
>> stth wrote:
>>> Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
>> Is there a non-joust melee for the lance?  The times I've tried it has
>> only worked like other poleaxes.  I hit with the hilt if if it were a
>> weak stick.  Or is applying it while not riding not jousting so it's
>> like using a poleam.
>>
> As per my knowledge, a lance behaves exactly like any other polearm when one 
> applies it while not riding. It is only when you melee an adjacent square 
> monster while riding does chance of jousting and lance breakage, factor into 
> the equation.

If I understand that correctly you need to be expert at lance before it
counts as a joust because only expert skill allows applying to adjacent
squares.  I think what confused me is exercising the weapon can happen
either on foot or riding.  Two squares forward at unskilled (would only
apply to a polearm for knights) or basic, using a knight's jump move if
skilled, anywhere within two squares if expert.  That's plenty of hits
before you can build up to a joust.  It rather matches with walking your
horse much of the time until it grows into a war horse through its own
combat experience, but riding it for the riding experience when
travelling for distance.  Something like explore and clear a level on
foot, move through a mostly cleared mostly explored level riding. 
Eventually riding more as it becomes necessary to increase riding
skills.

>> Since Excalibur is an artifact, there are no artifact lances, and
>> knights can two weapon I wonder about doing a lot of jousting.  Not an
>> option in dark or maze levels.  Great for both castles.  Is it that much
>> better than dual wield of Excalibur and a nice non-artifact melee weapon
>> most of the time?
>
> In a general sense, the answer is no I guess. If one is dual-weilding 
> Excalibur/Grayswandir and another longsword/silver saber, raw damage caused 
> by that combination should be greater than that caused by a joust.
> But there are other things to consider, jousting does not trigger passive 
> abilities so floating eyes, jellies, molds etc, can be safely dispatched via 
> melee combat. It also pushes monsters back a square and seems to stun them 
> so than one can act again before them. A lucky streak of jousts probably 
> means a monster can be killed without it ever getting a chance to retaliate.

Yet most of the passive attack monsters are popcorn by the time enough
skill slots come available and enough class hits have been accumulated. 
Interesting.  Thanks!


